[1m[95m[NARRATION][0m 
  The Copper Finch holds its morning gloom like a grudge. Rain ticks against 
  the shuttered windows. Copper lamps cast amber pools across scarred oak. Mara 
  Venn looks up from the table. Her charcoal coat rustles as she leans back, 
  fingers steepled. The sealed reliquary sits between you — iron, fist-sized, 
  that bronze pin glinting in the low light. "Kael." A nod. No warmth, no chill 
  — just the flat efficiency of someone who's been waiting for this 
  conversation. She taps the reliquary once. A dull *thunk*. "The Ash Gate 
  doesn't open anymore. Not since Old Bram died." She says his name like she's 
  reading a headstone. "The ferryman was the lock. Without him, the gate's just 
  dead iron and old wards." A pause. She watches you. "But dead things have a 
  way of waking up. Three nights ago, someone tried to force it. Left scorch 
  marks on the frame and a handful of ash that wasn't wood ash." She tilts her 
  head. "That's what I've learned. The question is what *you* know — and 
  whether you're ready to walk through what shouldn't open." 

