[1m[95m[NARRATION][0m 
  Kael's hand moves to his pack. Fingers find the familiar brass rim of the 
  compass, worn smooth by years of use. He draws it out — a pale disc in the 
  copper light, its needle swaying, settling. Mara's eyes track the motion. She 
  doesn't reach for it. Doesn't lean in. Instead, her gaze lifts to meet his. 
  Holds. The reliquary sits between them. Silent. The bronze pin catching the 
  lamplight like a single amber eye. "You're thinking about handing it over," 
  she says, voice low. "But you haven't decided yet. I can see it in your 
  shoulders." She doesn't move to take it. She waits. The rain picks up against 
  the shutters. A gust rattles the window frame. Somewhere deeper in the 
  tavern, a floorboard creaks — someone's awake, someone's listening. "You came 
  here with questions about the Ash Gate. I've answered what I can." Her thumb 
  traces the edge of the reliquary. "But trust isn't a coin you drop in a 
  stranger's palm. It's a door you open when you're sure what's on the other 
  side." She holds your gaze. "So ask yourself — what are you actually handing 
  me? A compass? Or the weight of where it's taken you?" 

