[1m[95m[NARRATION][0m 
  Mara's gloved hand dips into her coat. When it emerges, the brass compass 
  catches the lamplight — a small, worn disc that gleams like a second moon in 
  the dim tavern. She doesn't look at it. Her eyes stay on you. Then she sets 
  it on the table. Slides it across the scarred oak. It comes to rest an inch 
  from your hand, needle swaying, settling toward north. "I took it because I 
  needed to know you'd trust me with something real." A pause. "You did. I 
  don't need to hold it anymore." She pulls her hand back, folds her arms. The 
  rain drums against the shutters. Somewhere in the back room, a floorboard 
  settles — or someone shifts their weight, listening. The reliquary sits 
  between you both. The bronze pin catches the light. "Trust's a funny thing," 
  she says, quieter now. "You hand it over once, and it comes back different. 
  Heavier. Or lighter. Depends on what you do with it." She nods at the 
  compass. "Yours again. Where to?" 
