[1m[95m[NARRATION][0m 
  Mara's hand goes to an inner pocket of her charcoal coat. Not the same one 
  she pulled the compass from — deeper. Closer to her chest. When her hand 
  emerges, something dark catches the lamplight. A key. Cut from polished 
  obsidian — black as a starless sky, veined with threads of deep silver that 
  seem to shift as she turns it. The teeth are sharp, irregular, carved with a 
  precision that doesn't look like any smith's work. It looks *grown* rather 
  than forged. She sets it on the table. The obsidian clicks against the oak — 
  a sound heavier than its size suggests. "I found it near the gate. Same night 
  I got this." She touches her gloved palm. "It was wedged into a crack in the 
  stone frame, half-hidden by scorch marks. Someone dropped it. Or left it 
  there deliberately." She pushes it an inch toward you. "Take it. I've had my 
  look. Maybe your eyes see something mine missed." The key lies between you — 
  cool, dark, waiting. The silver veins catch the candlelight like captured 
  threads of moonlight. Your fingers close around it. It's colder than it 
  should be. Denser. It settles into your palm like it belongs there. Mara 
  watches you with an unreadable expression. "Keep it close. I've got a feeling 
  that key's going to matter more than either of us knows yet." The rain 
  hammers against the shutters. The reliquary's bronze pin glints beside the 
  empty space where the key just rested. Mara tilts her head toward the door. 
  "Ready to walk, or do you need a moment?" 
