[1m[95m[NARRATION][0m 
  The name leaves your mouth before you catch it — a reflex, a habit, the ghost 
  of a name still hanging in the air where a living man used to stand. "Bram." 
  Nothing answers. The tavern holds its silence. Rain drums the shutters. A 
  lamp hisses softly. The only response is the absence of one — the hollow 
  space where a ferryman's gruff voice should have been. And in that silence, 
  you remember. *Three nights ago. The Ash Gate.* Old Bram had been alive then. 
  Standing at the edge of the pilgrim's path, his lantern casting a circle of 
  weak light against the dark. He'd grabbed your arm — bony fingers, surprising 
  strength — and pulled you close enough to smell the pipe smoke and wood ash 
  on his coat. *"That gate ain't a door, boy. It's a wound. And there's things 
  on the other side that remember when the world was different. You go poking 
  at it, you're gonna draw attention — the kind that leaves marks."* He'd let 
  go. Stepped back. Shook his head. *"I've ferried folk to and from that 
  threshold for forty years. Never once stepped through. You want my advice? 
  Don't be the first."* The memory fades. The tavern is quiet. Old Bram is dead 
  — throat cut, found face-down in the mud fifty yards from the gate he'd 
  guarded all his life. And you're still here. Still holding a cold key in your 
  pack. Still heading toward a gate that someone died trying to force open. The 
  rain keeps falling. 

