[1m[95m[NARRATION][0m 
  The door swings shut behind you, cutting the tavern's amber glow to a thin 
  line, then nothing. Rain hits your face immediately. Cold. Needle-fine. The 
  street is a smear of mud and shadow, the pilgrim's path little more than a 
  rutted track vanishing into grey. You pull your collar up and walk. The 
  half-mile stretches. Mud sucks at your boots. The mist thickens ahead, 
  swallowing the edge of the road until there's no horizon — just wet stone, 
  wet earth, wet air pressing in from all sides. You pass the scorch marks 
  before you see the gate. A blackened patch on the stone wall to your left — 
  five feet wide, branching like cracked lightning. The stone beneath is 
  blistered. And at the base, a small heap of ash that the rain hasn't washed 
  away. Not wood-ash. Pale. Almost white. It clings to the stone in a way ash 
  shouldn't. Then the mist parts. The Ash Gate rises before you. A cracked 
  black arch, fifteen feet tall, set into a wall that shouldn't exist in this 
  part of the town. The stone is wrong — darker than the surrounding rock, 
  smoother, as if it was *poured* into place rather than cut. The arch frames 
  nothing but a sheet of absolute black, a darkness so complete it seems to 
  drink the grey daylight around it. The frame is scarred. Gashed. Fresh gouges 
  in the stone, as if something with claws tried to dig through. And there — 
  wedged into a crack near the right pillar — a fleck of obsidian, no bigger 
  than a fingernail. A chip. From a key that fits here. The air around the gate 
  is still. Colder than it should be. The rain seems to avoid the arch itself, 
  falling in a perfect curtain a foot from the black surface. And at the base 
  of the left pillar, half-hidden by mud and moss: a dark stain. Old blood. 
  Bram's blood. The gate waits. 

