[1m[95m[NARRATION][0m 
  The darkness of the gate pulses — a slow, rhythmic throb that has no source. 
  The air around it is dead. No wind touches this place. The rain slides off an 
  invisible dome a foot from the arch, leaving the ground beneath it bone-dry 
  and cracked. A figure steps out from behind the left pillar. You didn't see 
  her approach. Didn't hear her boots on the wet ground. She's simply *there* — 
  a woman in a dark blue courier's coat, the hood pulled low over her face. A 
  leather satchel is slung across her body, the strap worn smooth from use. 
  She's young, maybe mid-twenties, with a sharp jaw and darker skin. Her eyes 
  are pale grey, almost silver, and they study you with the flat patience of 
  someone who's been standing in one place for a long time. She doesn't 
  startle. Doesn't reach for a weapon. Just tilts her head, rain sliding off 
  her hood, and waits for you to speak. You give your name. Ask your question. 
  A pause. A beat of silence where the only sound is the rain hissing against 
  that invisible dome. Then she speaks. Her voice is low, measured — the voice 
  of someone used to delivering messages across dangerous ground. "Sable 
  Quill." She touches two fingers to her collar — a small brass pin shaped like 
  a folded letter. "I've been waiting since dawn. The message I carry is 
  addressed to the one who holds the obsidian key." Her grey eyes drop to your 
  pack. Then back to your face. "That's you, isn't it?" 

